WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:02.979
- Hi everyone, welcome to
the Homeroom livestream

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:04.829
Sal here from Khan Academy.

00:00:04.829 --> 00:00:06.509
We have a very exciting guest today,

00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:09.019
Margaret Spellings former
Secretary of Education

00:00:09.019 --> 00:00:13.879
of the United States
and CEO of Texas 2036.

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:15.349
But before we get to that,

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:17.409
I will get my standard announcements.

00:00:17.409 --> 00:00:19.289
First of all, reminder that Khan Academy

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:21.309
is a not for profit organization.

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:23.259
We can only exist through
philanthropic donations.

00:00:23.259 --> 00:00:24.949
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:28.509
please think about going
to Khan academy.org/donate.

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:30.829
I also want to give a special shout out

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:33.299
to several corporations
that have stepped up

00:00:33.299 --> 00:00:36.449
when they appreciated how
many people were leaning

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:38.789
on Khan Academy during the COVID crisis

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:40.339
and continue to lean on Khan Academy

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:41.879
and that we were already
running at a deficit

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:43.479
and our costs were going up.

00:00:43.479 --> 00:00:45.889
So Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:00:45.889 --> 00:00:47.709
AT&amp;T, fastly and Novartis

00:00:47.709 --> 00:00:50.019
and the many other
supporters of Khan Academy,

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:52.989
but we still have a gap,
especially as we go into 2021.

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:55.459
So as much help as you can provide

00:00:55.459 --> 00:00:56.709
is very much appreciated.

00:00:56.709 --> 00:00:58.349
I also want to remind everyone

00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:01.459
that you can get a version
of this live stream

00:01:01.459 --> 00:01:02.879
wherever you get your podcasts

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:05.829
Homeroom with Sal, The podcast.

00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:09.492
So with that, I'm excited to
introduce Secretary Spelling's.

00:01:10.619 --> 00:01:13.613
- Hi Sal thanks for
everything that you do.

00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:16.599
- No, thanks for joining us.

00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:18.319
Obviously it's a day

00:01:18.319 --> 00:01:19.939
where there's a lot going on in the world

00:01:19.939 --> 00:01:21.899
but it looks like the election results

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:23.109
are kind of in a pause mode

00:01:23.109 --> 00:01:25.502
so maybe it is a good time for us to chat.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:28.229
- About something else, yeah.

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:30.049
- Exactly about something else.

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:32.489
So there's a ton to talk about it

00:01:32.489 --> 00:01:34.229
and I want to encourage everyone watching

00:01:34.229 --> 00:01:36.969
to put any questions you
have for secretary Spellings

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:39.269
or myself on the message boards,

00:01:39.269 --> 00:01:41.489
we have team members
who will surface them,

00:01:41.489 --> 00:01:43.849
and I'll try to get to
as many as possible.

00:01:43.849 --> 00:01:45.539
But Margaret, there's I guess two things

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:46.619
I'd love to talk about.

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:49.069
Well, first of all, explain to all of us

00:01:49.069 --> 00:01:52.582
what Texas 2036 is.

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:56.749
- Well, 2036 is the bicentennial of Texas,

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:59.149
our 200th birthday and we use that kind of

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:01.899
as a hook to try to get people

00:02:01.899 --> 00:02:03.919
to think about the future.

00:02:03.919 --> 00:02:05.149
What we're trying to do is put

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:07.829
what I call the sensible center together

00:02:07.829 --> 00:02:10.889
to think long term about
the most important issues

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:12.409
that confront our state in the course,

00:02:12.409 --> 00:02:14.439
those are education and health,

00:02:14.439 --> 00:02:16.869
natural resources, infrastructure,

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:20.519
the way our government performs
justice and safety issues.

00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:24.199
And we're trying to corral
people to focus on the future,

00:02:24.199 --> 00:02:26.079
to think big, to think long term

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:28.469
and to use tons and tons of data,

00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:31.082
the facts to inform our work.

00:02:33.089 --> 00:02:37.319
- And is this like kind of
spawned by the government

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:40.884
or it's a separate kind of
industry, not for profit.

00:02:40.884 --> 00:02:44.479
- It's a not for profit
organization really created

00:02:44.479 --> 00:02:45.759
by the business community

00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:47.749
it's a who's who of business

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:50.099
and civic leaders from around our state,

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:53.679
who want us to get out of
the tyranny of the urgent,

00:02:53.679 --> 00:02:56.689
and think long term about
what our state will need.

00:02:56.689 --> 00:02:59.359
We know Texas like California and Nevada,

00:02:59.359 --> 00:03:04.359
the West where we operate,
is big and growing

00:03:04.819 --> 00:03:06.789
and getting more diverse by the day,

00:03:06.789 --> 00:03:08.419
we're getting older,
we're getting younger,

00:03:08.419 --> 00:03:11.819
we have a great hand to
play with human capital

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:14.139
as our number one asset.

00:03:14.139 --> 00:03:15.719
But we have to do a lot of things right

00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:18.899
to make sure that we fulfill them

00:03:18.899 --> 00:03:21.529
and leverage the possibility
that we have with

00:03:21.529 --> 00:03:24.379
this vast number of talented
people we have in our state.

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:29.627
- Paint a picture for us
what is Texas 2036 look like

00:03:29.627 --> 00:03:31.202
If y'all are successful?

00:03:32.179 --> 00:03:36.349
- Well, we will have invested
in education and health,

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:38.979
we sadly lead the nation in the number

00:03:38.979 --> 00:03:42.679
of uninsured, adults and children.

00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:45.709
We have the health outcomes to show for it

00:03:45.709 --> 00:03:50.509
we are dead last on things
like maternal mortality

00:03:50.509 --> 00:03:53.249
and diabetes and obesity
and things like that

00:03:53.249 --> 00:03:55.579
when it comes to health indicators.

00:03:55.579 --> 00:03:57.699
Where we were once in the mid 30s,

00:03:57.699 --> 00:04:00.719
on our national education,
report card and reading,

00:04:00.719 --> 00:04:04.019
we have fallen to the low 40s on the nape

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:06.609
and reading and so we have work to do.

00:04:06.609 --> 00:04:09.199
And it's gonna take vigilance

00:04:09.199 --> 00:04:11.179
and attention by the business community.

00:04:11.179 --> 00:04:13.099
It's gonna take resources,

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:15.389
but it's gonna take really
all of us understanding

00:04:15.389 --> 00:04:18.149
that these are the
priorities for our state

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:21.839
and for our nation and
we have to attend to them

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:24.539
in the short term and over the long haul.

00:04:24.539 --> 00:04:25.952
Rome wasn't built in a day.

00:04:27.369 --> 00:04:30.039
- Absolutely, and putting your education

00:04:30.039 --> 00:04:32.119
which is really, part of education 2036

00:04:32.119 --> 00:04:35.569
and obviously, your experiences
as Secretary of Education,

00:04:35.569 --> 00:04:38.259
Whoever is the next
administration, obviously,

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:41.839
we've had an, beyond
the healthcare crisis,

00:04:41.839 --> 00:04:44.029
the economic crisis, we've
had an education crisis

00:04:44.029 --> 00:04:46.209
because of distance learning and COVID.

00:04:46.209 --> 00:04:47.329
One could argue we already had

00:04:47.329 --> 00:04:50.049
an education crisis even before COVID.

00:04:50.049 --> 00:04:52.259
If you were advising whoever

00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:55.179
is going to be Secretary
of Education come January,

00:04:55.179 --> 00:04:57.499
what would you encourage them to focus on

00:04:57.499 --> 00:04:59.252
and how to approach it?

00:05:00.689 --> 00:05:02.749
- Well, one of the things
I think is so critical,

00:05:02.749 --> 00:05:04.629
and you've just said it, Sal is that,

00:05:04.629 --> 00:05:07.269
our students have really lost ground.

00:05:07.269 --> 00:05:09.389
And in some cases, we've lost the kids.

00:05:09.389 --> 00:05:12.995
I mean, literally, our
enrollment in Texas is down 6%

00:05:12.995 --> 00:05:15.499
those numbers hold up across the country.

00:05:15.499 --> 00:05:18.369
We have probably two to
four million students

00:05:18.369 --> 00:05:22.299
who were enrolled last
year and aren't this year,

00:05:22.299 --> 00:05:23.829
mostly in the early grades

00:05:23.829 --> 00:05:25.889
and pre-K programs in the like.

00:05:25.889 --> 00:05:30.249
For those students that
are enrolled and learning,

00:05:30.249 --> 00:05:33.739
we know that it's not as
productive as it once was,

00:05:33.739 --> 00:05:37.289
obviously, with an inability
often to meet face to face

00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:39.659
and engage with teachers and peers.

00:05:39.659 --> 00:05:42.839
And so we really have to
confront learning loss,

00:05:42.839 --> 00:05:46.819
we cannot afford to lose
this generation of kids.

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:49.169
And they are way behind overall,

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:51.429
especially our poor and minority students,

00:05:51.429 --> 00:05:54.409
those students for whom
COVID has revealed,

00:05:54.409 --> 00:05:56.919
really a incredible amount of inequity.

00:05:56.919 --> 00:05:59.943
And so what I would do if I were advising

00:05:59.943 --> 00:06:02.549
the next president, is to say,

00:06:02.549 --> 00:06:05.269
and your tailor made for this Sal

00:06:05.269 --> 00:06:07.099
we've got to think about how are we going

00:06:07.099 --> 00:06:08.859
to get our students caught up

00:06:08.859 --> 00:06:10.459
and how are you gonna use tutoring

00:06:10.459 --> 00:06:14.589
and one-on-one coaching
and other interventions,

00:06:14.589 --> 00:06:18.789
research based practices to
get those kids caught up.

00:06:18.789 --> 00:06:20.319
Secondly, I would say we have

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:22.589
to have broadband, ubiquitous broadband

00:06:22.589 --> 00:06:25.439
and devices everywhere, period.

00:06:25.439 --> 00:06:27.369
And we need a national plan to do that,

00:06:27.369 --> 00:06:30.459
obviously, states are
important in those discussions.

00:06:30.459 --> 00:06:32.809
So those are two things
I would do right away.

00:06:34.099 --> 00:06:37.759
- And actually, I'd also
love you to advise us

00:06:37.759 --> 00:06:39.289
because your first point, actually,

00:06:39.289 --> 00:06:40.409
it touches on both points.

00:06:40.409 --> 00:06:41.955
But on that first point about

00:06:41.955 --> 00:06:44.489
how do you fill in folks gaps,

00:06:44.489 --> 00:06:46.139
do the necessary mediation so that

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:48.362
this year's damage doesn't last,

00:06:49.329 --> 00:06:51.299
that's what we work on a
lot here at Khan Academy

00:06:51.299 --> 00:06:54.769
but I'll be very open when I
think about interfacing it kind

00:06:54.769 --> 00:06:57.449
of a governmental level,
especially the federal government,

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:58.919
it feels very intimidating.

00:06:58.919 --> 00:07:00.479
How do you get these two worlds

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:02.949
to kind of work together?

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:05.169
Do you think there are
opportunities there?

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:06.002
- I sure do.

00:07:06.002 --> 00:07:10.419
There are platforms like
AmeriCorps and City Year

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:14.119
and programs like that,
that could be built out

00:07:14.119 --> 00:07:16.989
to engage a workforce if you will,

00:07:16.989 --> 00:07:19.789
that can be engaged in
this tutoring and coaching.

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:21.419
We know, here's the problem.

00:07:21.419 --> 00:07:23.899
Kids have have lost ground.

00:07:23.899 --> 00:07:25.259
And second, we know we need

00:07:25.259 --> 00:07:27.369
to intervene with them in safe ways.

00:07:27.369 --> 00:07:28.309
Well, how do we do that?

00:07:28.309 --> 00:07:31.569
Well, it's going to be one on
one engagement, often online,

00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:33.909
sometimes in person, can be in person,

00:07:33.909 --> 00:07:36.059
we could do that safely, potentially.

00:07:36.059 --> 00:07:37.559
How do we get them trained?

00:07:37.559 --> 00:07:39.479
And with research based practices,

00:07:39.479 --> 00:07:42.239
how do we identify the kids
that need help the most?

00:07:42.239 --> 00:07:45.439
And how do we use platforms
like AmeriCorps and others

00:07:45.439 --> 00:07:49.692
to set sail and join hands
on this on this issue?

00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:53.329
I think people, whether
they're retired people,

00:07:53.329 --> 00:07:55.139
or university students, people want

00:07:55.139 --> 00:07:56.932
to help our students catch up.

00:07:58.466 --> 00:07:59.469
- And so you're kind of imagining a world

00:07:59.469 --> 00:08:02.659
where AmeriCorps or maybe even
a broader kind of coalition

00:08:02.659 --> 00:08:04.509
of volunteers are actually going in there

00:08:04.509 --> 00:08:07.526
and providing the the one-on-one help,

00:08:07.526 --> 00:08:08.489
that a lot of kids are going to,

00:08:08.489 --> 00:08:10.939
arguably always needed,
but need even more now.

00:08:10.939 --> 00:08:14.129
- Exactly, and I think we
have existing platforms

00:08:14.129 --> 00:08:15.719
of both at the state and federal level

00:08:15.719 --> 00:08:17.099
that can be deployed to do that.

00:08:17.099 --> 00:08:18.369
We can pay some of these people,

00:08:18.369 --> 00:08:20.119
maybe there are some that are volunteers.

00:08:20.119 --> 00:08:21.149
What we know for sure,

00:08:21.149 --> 00:08:23.669
is that these individuals will need skills

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:26.959
and training at a sophisticated level

00:08:26.959 --> 00:08:29.299
to help kids get caught up, obviously,

00:08:29.299 --> 00:08:32.019
it's easier to do and
with younger children,

00:08:32.019 --> 00:08:32.852
then if you're trying

00:08:32.852 --> 00:08:34.889
to help kids get caught up in calculus,

00:08:34.889 --> 00:08:37.976
you have to know something
about calculus, like you Sal.

00:08:40.359 --> 00:08:41.719
- No absolutely.

00:08:42.569 --> 00:08:44.259
It's interesting, because
obviously, Khan Academy

00:08:44.259 --> 00:08:46.539
is one layer where people can
get practice at their own time

00:08:46.539 --> 00:08:48.539
and pace and fill in those gaps.

00:08:48.539 --> 00:08:52.229
And one idea that your
your last comment just kind

00:08:52.229 --> 00:08:53.879
of triggered in my mind is maybe working

00:08:53.879 --> 00:08:55.069
with a lot of these groups,

00:08:55.069 --> 00:08:57.119
so that they can be kind of a force

00:08:57.119 --> 00:08:59.169
that engages a lot of these students

00:08:59.169 --> 00:09:00.379
on platforms like Khan Academy,

00:09:00.379 --> 00:09:03.689
but also the opportunity to do small group

00:09:03.689 --> 00:09:04.659
or one-on-one tutoring.

00:09:04.659 --> 00:09:05.859
There's a separate not for profit

00:09:05.859 --> 00:09:07.969
that we've just spawned up
that I've just spawned up

00:09:07.969 --> 00:09:10.419
it's separate from Khan Academy
called Schoolhouse.world,

00:09:10.419 --> 00:09:12.719
which is focused on,
how do we pair students

00:09:12.719 --> 00:09:15.209
for free with live tutoring.

00:09:15.209 --> 00:09:17.679
So I'd love to talk to you off offline

00:09:17.679 --> 00:09:20.489
about how all of these different pieces

00:09:20.489 --> 00:09:23.139
can fit together on the digital divide,

00:09:23.139 --> 00:09:24.469
which is kind of your second big point,

00:09:24.469 --> 00:09:26.289
which I couldn't agree with more.

00:09:26.289 --> 00:09:27.149
What do you think?

00:09:27.149 --> 00:09:28.542
Well, first question,

00:09:29.432 --> 00:09:31.289
why don't you think this was part of

00:09:31.289 --> 00:09:32.859
maybe the first few rounds of stimulus,

00:09:32.859 --> 00:09:34.969
it feels like a no brainer to me,

00:09:34.969 --> 00:09:38.089
for 10 or $20 billion,
out of a trillion or two.

00:09:38.089 --> 00:09:40.779
You could maybe solve this problem?

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:43.199
And do you have hopes that it will happen

00:09:43.199 --> 00:09:45.323
in the next few rounds of stimulus?

00:09:45.323 --> 00:09:49.029
- It's a complicated problem,
every state is different,

00:09:49.029 --> 00:09:52.109
the provider community, the
private sector community

00:09:52.109 --> 00:09:54.689
is different in each state,

00:09:54.689 --> 00:09:57.069
whether they have attacked this through

00:09:57.069 --> 00:09:59.699
some sort of a central
planning process or a hub

00:10:00.599 --> 00:10:02.319
that varies all over the country.

00:10:02.319 --> 00:10:05.419
So I know that we in Texas
are one of just six states

00:10:05.419 --> 00:10:08.149
that don't have a plan,
which limits our ability

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:10.349
to draw down federal resources.

00:10:10.349 --> 00:10:13.709
But when I ask why is that
it's because we're big,

00:10:13.709 --> 00:10:15.939
we have a multitude of providers,

00:10:15.939 --> 00:10:19.389
we have, not the bottom
of the Grand Canyon,

00:10:19.389 --> 00:10:20.469
but the Rio Grande Valley,

00:10:20.469 --> 00:10:22.119
I mean, very, very remote places.

00:10:22.119 --> 00:10:23.669
And then, of course,
what we know for sure,

00:10:23.669 --> 00:10:25.099
and you know this

00:10:25.099 --> 00:10:29.459
is that in urban America, this
is an affordability issue.

00:10:29.459 --> 00:10:31.689
It's not that the
infrastructure doesn't exist,

00:10:31.689 --> 00:10:34.739
it's that folks can't afford
the hundreds of dollars,

00:10:34.739 --> 00:10:37.642
often it is for those
high speed connections.

00:10:38.969 --> 00:10:41.629
- And how would you
recommend we can solve this?

00:10:41.629 --> 00:10:42.909
Is this something that does get solved

00:10:42.909 --> 00:10:43.969
to the federal government level

00:10:43.969 --> 00:10:45.989
you put in the stimulus
program or something else?

00:10:45.989 --> 00:10:48.349
Or is it is this more
of a state level issue?

00:10:48.349 --> 00:10:50.719
- I think it's going to
have to be a combination

00:10:50.719 --> 00:10:52.739
of both, I mean, some federal policies,

00:10:52.739 --> 00:10:56.519
some freeing up of options for states,

00:10:56.519 --> 00:10:59.529
but states are fundamentally
going to have to attack it.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:01.439
Obviously, we need additional resources

00:11:01.439 --> 00:11:02.849
from the federal government,

00:11:02.849 --> 00:11:04.409
we in the States cannot print money

00:11:04.409 --> 00:11:06.329
for these sorts of things like they can.

00:11:06.329 --> 00:11:08.429
But if we're gonna address it quickly,

00:11:08.429 --> 00:11:09.979
it'll need to be a partnership.

00:11:11.179 --> 00:11:12.469
- And as we go through this crisis,

00:11:12.469 --> 00:11:14.959
obviously, a lot of sub optimal things,

00:11:14.959 --> 00:11:16.119
we've talked about several of them,

00:11:16.119 --> 00:11:17.709
a lot of kids might fall further behind

00:11:17.709 --> 00:11:19.119
they've been disengaged,

00:11:19.119 --> 00:11:22.429
do you see any potential silver linings

00:11:22.429 --> 00:11:24.489
from what we're going through right now?

00:11:24.489 --> 00:11:26.459
Or reasons for hope?

00:11:26.459 --> 00:11:28.819
- Yeah, I used that same phrase

00:11:28.819 --> 00:11:30.729
and Arne Duncan corrected me the other day

00:11:30.729 --> 00:11:32.019
and said, these are not silver linings,

00:11:32.019 --> 00:11:33.189
they're hard lessons.

00:11:33.189 --> 00:11:34.979
And I agree with that.

00:11:34.979 --> 00:11:39.214
But I do think there are some takeaways,

00:11:39.214 --> 00:11:41.519
that in some learnings from this

00:11:41.519 --> 00:11:45.709
one is, I think parents are
have a better understanding

00:11:45.709 --> 00:11:47.919
of where their students are,

00:11:47.919 --> 00:11:51.409
it's one thing to get a report
card home in the backpack

00:11:51.409 --> 00:11:54.839
and it's a whole other thing
to see your child working

00:11:54.839 --> 00:11:57.769
and struggling and maybe
not reading on grade level

00:11:57.769 --> 00:12:01.149
or with the, needed math
skills, or whatever.

00:12:01.149 --> 00:12:04.489
So I think parents have some
insights they didn't before.

00:12:04.489 --> 00:12:07.709
Secondly, I think, we've
made much more progress

00:12:07.709 --> 00:12:11.859
in technology, learning
and having our teachers,

00:12:11.859 --> 00:12:13.549
yes, struggle with it,

00:12:13.549 --> 00:12:15.519
but make progress and understand

00:12:15.519 --> 00:12:19.239
how we could better maximize this tool.

00:12:19.239 --> 00:12:22.039
Third, I think we're learning and seeing

00:12:22.039 --> 00:12:25.339
that it can be a tool where customization

00:12:25.339 --> 00:12:28.749
and embedded assessment
progress monitoring,

00:12:28.749 --> 00:12:33.739
can be, standard operating procedure.

00:12:33.739 --> 00:12:35.619
So I think there are something,

00:12:35.619 --> 00:12:37.749
we're also learning that our human capital

00:12:37.749 --> 00:12:41.389
can be deployed in more
efficient, smarter ways.

00:12:41.389 --> 00:12:44.359
Maybe we need to have
the world's best lecturer

00:12:44.359 --> 00:12:46.889
doing the teaching remotely,

00:12:46.889 --> 00:12:49.609
while others are doing coaching

00:12:49.609 --> 00:12:52.489
and one-on-one, meetings with parents

00:12:52.489 --> 00:12:53.969
and families and students and the like.

00:12:53.969 --> 00:12:56.029
So I think we are learning some things,

00:12:56.029 --> 00:12:59.219
we're seeing a
proliferation of innovation,

00:12:59.219 --> 00:13:01.519
and problem solving around the country.

00:13:01.519 --> 00:13:03.329
And one thing I do think is missing

00:13:03.329 --> 00:13:04.829
that I wish we would would do

00:13:04.829 --> 00:13:08.239
is engage our universities to go establish

00:13:08.239 --> 00:13:10.809
a research agenda so that
at the end of the day,

00:13:10.809 --> 00:13:12.739
at the end of this big experiment,

00:13:12.739 --> 00:13:15.739
we've learned some things about
what worked and what didn't.

00:13:16.739 --> 00:13:18.319
- I love that last idea, because yeah,

00:13:18.319 --> 00:13:19.919
there's always,
Universities always looking

00:13:19.919 --> 00:13:20.752
for things to research,

00:13:20.752 --> 00:13:22.833
and we have a massive
experiment going on right now.

00:13:22.833 --> 00:13:24.509
- Yes we do.

00:13:24.509 --> 00:13:26.439
- That seems worth study.

00:13:26.439 --> 00:13:29.469
So getting a lot of questions
in from a social media

00:13:29.469 --> 00:13:32.229
from YouTube Park Pal, is asking,

00:13:32.229 --> 00:13:34.689
what's it like being
Secretary of Education?

00:13:34.689 --> 00:13:38.459
What do Secretaries of Education do?

00:13:38.459 --> 00:13:40.356
And yeah I'll just add to that question.

00:13:40.356 --> 00:13:41.909
- It's a good question.

00:13:41.909 --> 00:13:43.349
- We imagine, in other countries,

00:13:43.349 --> 00:13:45.469
we would call it a Minister of Education.

00:13:45.469 --> 00:13:46.859
What is the role?

00:13:46.859 --> 00:13:50.089
How's it special in the
United States especially?

00:13:50.089 --> 00:13:53.359
- Well, it's an important platform.

00:13:53.359 --> 00:13:57.849
And I think, and I'll
leave to the audience,

00:13:57.849 --> 00:14:01.639
how well it's been maximized
in this recent past.

00:14:01.639 --> 00:14:04.199
But, if I were the Secretary of Education,

00:14:04.199 --> 00:14:08.159
I would be gathering experts
from all of these fields,

00:14:08.159 --> 00:14:13.029
mental health, technology,
disease control,

00:14:13.029 --> 00:14:16.429
space utilization, I never called anybody

00:14:16.429 --> 00:14:19.219
from the White House or from
the US Department of Education

00:14:19.219 --> 00:14:22.409
and say, I need your help where
they didn't come in running.

00:14:22.409 --> 00:14:25.579
And so I think it can be
a convener of expertise,

00:14:25.579 --> 00:14:29.099
a proliferator of
information and practice.

00:14:29.099 --> 00:14:33.089
It can be a great listening
agency as a department

00:14:33.089 --> 00:14:36.969
as people are struggling with
and learning from what happens

00:14:36.969 --> 00:14:40.459
so to use that bully pulpit
in really muscular ways,

00:14:40.459 --> 00:14:42.939
is a big part of the job.

00:14:42.939 --> 00:14:47.499
Secondly, it can be an
advocate for resources,

00:14:47.499 --> 00:14:50.449
both within the administration
and in the Congress

00:14:50.449 --> 00:14:55.449
and a translator of what's
happening around the country

00:14:55.689 --> 00:14:58.559
and the solution provider,

00:14:58.559 --> 00:15:01.329
so that the Congress can act.

00:15:01.329 --> 00:15:05.429
And, I think we could do a
better job of doing that too,

00:15:05.429 --> 00:15:07.729
especially on things like broadband,

00:15:07.729 --> 00:15:10.389
what are the consequences
for not having broadband

00:15:10.389 --> 00:15:13.149
in our schools, both in
terms of learning loss,

00:15:13.149 --> 00:15:16.489
but in terms of health care and a lack

00:15:16.489 --> 00:15:18.879
of economic connectedness and the like.

00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:23.559
So it's basically an
information proliferator,

00:15:23.559 --> 00:15:27.469
a listener and a gatherer of
expertise on the policy level.

00:15:27.469 --> 00:15:29.389
And then secondly, and most importantly,

00:15:29.389 --> 00:15:32.429
especially in higher education,
certainly in K12 too,

00:15:32.429 --> 00:15:35.949
it is a distributor of native resources,

00:15:35.949 --> 00:15:38.499
especially around issues of equity,

00:15:38.499 --> 00:15:43.199
Title 1 schools,
financially needy students

00:15:43.199 --> 00:15:44.912
in higher education and the like.

00:15:46.239 --> 00:15:49.549
- On that last, on that kind
of policy, budgetary front,

00:15:49.549 --> 00:15:51.499
where do you see the biggest levers?

00:15:51.499 --> 00:15:53.919
If you were advising the next President,

00:15:53.919 --> 00:15:57.072
where would you try to make
sure you see extra investment?

00:15:58.929 --> 00:16:00.989
- Well, I think, one of
the things of course,

00:16:00.989 --> 00:16:02.479
you would expect me to say this

00:16:02.479 --> 00:16:05.789
as a strong advocate for assessment

00:16:05.789 --> 00:16:08.299
and measurement and transparency,

00:16:08.299 --> 00:16:11.269
that no child left behind, started,

00:16:11.269 --> 00:16:15.539
and that was affirmed by the
Every Student Succeeds Act.

00:16:15.539 --> 00:16:17.379
And that is that we have to,

00:16:17.379 --> 00:16:19.269
we will not solve issues of equity

00:16:19.269 --> 00:16:22.659
if we bury our heads in the
sand and stop caring enough

00:16:22.659 --> 00:16:24.799
to find out, as I like to say.

00:16:24.799 --> 00:16:28.589
So how do we think about
measurement accountability

00:16:28.589 --> 00:16:30.589
and testing in this era?

00:16:30.589 --> 00:16:31.889
What's appropriate?

00:16:31.889 --> 00:16:32.769
How do we do it?

00:16:32.769 --> 00:16:33.949
How do we collect data?

00:16:33.949 --> 00:16:36.899
How do we still have that accountability?

00:16:36.899 --> 00:16:40.569
We taxpayers are paying a big load

00:16:40.569 --> 00:16:42.179
for educating our students.

00:16:42.179 --> 00:16:45.086
And yes, these are challenging times,

00:16:45.086 --> 00:16:47.879
but how might we think about
those issues going forward?

00:16:47.879 --> 00:16:51.069
I'm a big, what gets measured
gets done kind of person.

00:16:51.069 --> 00:16:53.199
And I think that's a major issue

00:16:53.199 --> 00:16:56.682
before policymakers and educators now.

00:16:57.799 --> 00:16:59.129
- And how do we thread that needle?

00:16:59.129 --> 00:17:01.799
'Cause it makes tons of
sense, anytime someone says,

00:17:01.799 --> 00:17:04.069
it's very popular to beat
up standardized assessment,

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:04.902
and I always say,

00:17:04.902 --> 00:17:07.429
would you prefer non
standardized assessment?

00:17:07.429 --> 00:17:09.909
Would you prefer no assessment?

00:17:09.909 --> 00:17:12.562
Because it seems like you need it.

00:17:13.999 --> 00:17:16.719
But, yeah, maybe some
people would agree with that

00:17:16.719 --> 00:17:19.289
but it does seem like, it's
an interesting question

00:17:19.289 --> 00:17:22.599
of how it is used, or how it is perceived.

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:24.529
There's a lot of debate right now,

00:17:24.529 --> 00:17:27.519
at the college admissions
level around schools

00:17:27.519 --> 00:17:28.949
going testing optional,

00:17:28.949 --> 00:17:30.859
that may be out of
necessity, because of COVID.

00:17:30.859 --> 00:17:33.859
But some, University of California system,

00:17:33.859 --> 00:17:35.589
just like, literally said,

00:17:35.589 --> 00:17:39.379
we will not consider ACTs or SATs.

00:17:39.379 --> 00:17:41.469
How do you think this is going to evolve

00:17:41.469 --> 00:17:43.619
and where do you think
it should evolve to?

00:17:44.709 --> 00:17:47.659
- Well, what we know is
that no system is perfect.

00:17:47.659 --> 00:17:50.549
And as you say, a lot of
higher ed institutions

00:17:50.549 --> 00:17:51.929
are test optional.

00:17:51.929 --> 00:17:55.409
Well, in my view, it's
just a matter of time,

00:17:55.409 --> 00:17:58.069
before things that are less standardized,

00:17:58.069 --> 00:18:03.029
less validated, more
subjective, come into question

00:18:03.029 --> 00:18:06.999
about fairness,
discrimination, and whatnot.

00:18:06.999 --> 00:18:09.819
So I like to think that,

00:18:09.819 --> 00:18:13.349
what we need is a full
and complete picture,

00:18:13.349 --> 00:18:17.349
of which standardized
measurement is a part

00:18:17.349 --> 00:18:21.659
and certainly that's true
in K12 education as well.

00:18:21.659 --> 00:18:23.379
Where I think we are getting,

00:18:23.379 --> 00:18:25.659
and this is what technology will bring us

00:18:25.659 --> 00:18:29.879
is embedded progress
monitoring along the way,

00:18:29.879 --> 00:18:32.919
that gives teachers real time information,

00:18:32.919 --> 00:18:36.329
so that they can tailor their instruction,

00:18:36.329 --> 00:18:39.889
they can intervene with
students, along the way,

00:18:39.889 --> 00:18:42.389
one of the things that
was, I guess, a flaw,

00:18:42.389 --> 00:18:47.389
although, the industry was where
it was, back in those days,

00:18:48.629 --> 00:18:51.179
the teachers didn't see
the value in assessment,

00:18:51.179 --> 00:18:53.349
because it took too long
to get any information

00:18:53.349 --> 00:18:55.309
to them that was useful.

00:18:55.309 --> 00:18:58.879
And so I think technology
will help us solve those gaps.

00:18:58.879 --> 00:19:01.379
Course correct and it'll
be better for kids,

00:19:01.379 --> 00:19:03.069
it'll be better for teachers

00:19:03.069 --> 00:19:08.019
and I hope that this high
stakes notion will die down,

00:19:08.019 --> 00:19:10.829
it's real high stakes for students to lose

00:19:10.829 --> 00:19:13.342
as much educational ground
as they're losing now.

00:19:14.339 --> 00:19:16.139
- And explain the term progress monitoring

00:19:16.139 --> 00:19:16.972
a little bit more.

00:19:16.972 --> 00:19:19.239
It's a term for those
who were in education,

00:19:19.239 --> 00:19:21.339
it's a term that's very
fashionable these days.

00:19:21.339 --> 00:19:23.749
What does it mean to you?

00:19:23.749 --> 00:19:26.079
- It means that you're
checking in on students

00:19:26.079 --> 00:19:30.179
in a regular way, and course
correcting along the way.

00:19:30.179 --> 00:19:34.299
So just like you when I was in
school back in the dark ages,

00:19:34.299 --> 00:19:36.489
you had your weekly spelling check

00:19:36.489 --> 00:19:38.459
or your weekly pop quiz or your,

00:19:38.459 --> 00:19:41.709
so it's a regular assessment,

00:19:41.709 --> 00:19:46.709
a low stakes embedded pop in
online that gives teachers

00:19:46.769 --> 00:19:49.072
and students information
about where they are.

00:19:50.704 --> 00:19:53.229
- In the way we imagined it is,

00:19:53.229 --> 00:19:54.959
as you mentioned, could
be on a paper base,

00:19:54.959 --> 00:19:55.809
it doesn't need technology.

00:19:55.809 --> 00:19:57.529
But on something like Khan Academy,

00:19:57.529 --> 00:20:00.529
every interaction is not just practice,

00:20:00.529 --> 00:20:02.089
it's also an assessment, you can log it,

00:20:02.089 --> 00:20:03.829
and we give the reports to teachers.

00:20:03.829 --> 00:20:04.849
And if they can look at that

00:20:04.849 --> 00:20:08.749
in real time, they can make
the appropriate interventions

00:20:08.749 --> 00:20:10.126
and do that type of thing.

00:20:10.126 --> 00:20:12.919
And to your point, in
that type of a world,

00:20:12.919 --> 00:20:14.389
you don't have to have that,

00:20:14.389 --> 00:20:16.219
you got one shot at this student,

00:20:16.219 --> 00:20:19.259
and this is gonna be a grade
for the term depending on

00:20:19.259 --> 00:20:22.329
how you do if you haven't
done so well the first go,

00:20:22.329 --> 00:20:24.870
keep working on it, and not a big deal.

00:20:24.870 --> 00:20:26.079
- Exactly, that's the
other thing that I think

00:20:26.079 --> 00:20:28.889
is gonna be a silver
lining out of this is,

00:20:28.889 --> 00:20:31.269
we are learning and we already knew this,

00:20:31.269 --> 00:20:34.459
but we'll come to expect that,

00:20:34.459 --> 00:20:36.599
education is more all a cart,

00:20:36.599 --> 00:20:38.869
that some students are
gonna master something

00:20:38.869 --> 00:20:41.529
in three months, some
it will take 13 months.

00:20:41.529 --> 00:20:43.979
But that this customization,

00:20:43.979 --> 00:20:47.379
because of the embedded
progress monitoring,

00:20:47.379 --> 00:20:49.479
and because the use of technology coupled

00:20:49.479 --> 00:20:51.539
with great educators

00:20:51.539 --> 00:20:54.362
will really change this model, I hope.

00:20:55.819 --> 00:20:58.149
- So, we talked a lot about 2036

00:20:58.149 --> 00:21:00.649
from a Texas lens, but I'll
stay indexed on that year,

00:21:00.649 --> 00:21:03.379
since that's a lot of focus of your life.

00:21:03.379 --> 00:21:05.932
How do you think especially
the education world,

00:21:06.779 --> 00:21:08.969
we talk a little bit how
Texas might be different 2036.

00:21:08.969 --> 00:21:11.959
But how do you think education
needs to be different

00:21:11.959 --> 00:21:13.089
or how do you think it will be different

00:21:13.089 --> 00:21:15.342
if we go fast forward 15 or 16 years?

00:21:16.519 --> 00:21:19.729
- I think we have to start
thinking more seriously

00:21:19.729 --> 00:21:24.729
about how we use time, different from now,

00:21:25.179 --> 00:21:27.919
the academic year has been blown up.

00:21:27.919 --> 00:21:30.829
The Academic Calendar has been blown up.

00:21:30.829 --> 00:21:34.829
And so how do we use our time in ways

00:21:34.829 --> 00:21:39.139
that are more all a cart, my word,

00:21:39.139 --> 00:21:41.999
and we have things like
stackable credentials,

00:21:41.999 --> 00:21:46.642
and badging and proof of
mastery along a continuum.

00:21:47.569 --> 00:21:50.389
And you just put that in your portfolio

00:21:50.389 --> 00:21:52.649
or your backpack or whatever.

00:21:52.649 --> 00:21:55.019
So that you're evidencing
your learning along the way.

00:21:55.019 --> 00:21:56.789
I think one thing that I'm seeing,

00:21:56.789 --> 00:21:59.039
and this is certainly
happening in higher education,

00:21:59.039 --> 00:22:01.239
and certainly in the employer community,

00:22:01.239 --> 00:22:04.409
brand is starting to matter much less,

00:22:04.409 --> 00:22:06.369
the name of the institution on the top

00:22:06.369 --> 00:22:09.449
of your diploma matters less now than,

00:22:09.449 --> 00:22:10.319
can you code?

00:22:10.319 --> 00:22:12.019
Can you think critically?

00:22:12.019 --> 00:22:12.919
Can you write?

00:22:12.919 --> 00:22:15.469
Can you, this, that, the other thing.

00:22:15.469 --> 00:22:17.929
And I think we, in education

00:22:17.929 --> 00:22:20.219
and organizations like yours Sal,

00:22:20.219 --> 00:22:23.049
are going to have to help translate

00:22:23.049 --> 00:22:24.869
does this person have a skill?

00:22:24.869 --> 00:22:26.719
Does this person have this knowledge?

00:22:28.229 --> 00:22:29.659
- No, you're absolutely, I mean,

00:22:29.659 --> 00:22:33.199
I also share that dream that,

00:22:33.199 --> 00:22:37.199
in the next 5, 10 15 years,
pathways are not based

00:22:37.199 --> 00:22:39.489
on seat time, there's alternative pathways

00:22:39.489 --> 00:22:41.589
they're based on competency

00:22:41.589 --> 00:22:43.979
and you can kind of clutch
together your own experience,

00:22:43.979 --> 00:22:48.699
and they can directly have
pathways to whatever folks want.

00:22:48.699 --> 00:22:50.519
Just in the remaining time we have,

00:22:50.519 --> 00:22:52.569
we have a lot of educators watching this,

00:22:52.569 --> 00:22:53.549
we have a lot of parents,

00:22:53.549 --> 00:22:57.189
we have a lot of students
any kind of thoughts

00:22:57.189 --> 00:22:59.119
that you, any advice you'd have for them,

00:22:59.119 --> 00:23:01.459
while as they tried to navigate their own

00:23:01.459 --> 00:23:03.119
or their children's
education through COVID.

00:23:03.119 --> 00:23:05.119
and then think about the future broadly.

00:23:06.509 --> 00:23:09.369
- Well, I do think there's no harm,

00:23:09.369 --> 00:23:12.569
whether you're a policymaker
in a state legislature,

00:23:12.569 --> 00:23:14.279
or a mom or dad with a student

00:23:14.279 --> 00:23:17.199
or a teacher, understanding,

00:23:17.199 --> 00:23:19.199
how are we aligning our performance

00:23:19.199 --> 00:23:21.239
with our use of resources.

00:23:21.239 --> 00:23:22.759
And what I like to say is

00:23:22.759 --> 00:23:24.759
we gotta put our money
where our mouths are,

00:23:24.759 --> 00:23:28.369
we say it's important to
close the achievement gap

00:23:28.369 --> 00:23:31.719
and to make sure that all
students reach a particular level.

00:23:31.719 --> 00:23:33.529
That was the whole, genius,

00:23:33.529 --> 00:23:35.739
if you will, behind No Child Left Behind,

00:23:35.739 --> 00:23:38.129
and having those higher expectations

00:23:38.129 --> 00:23:39.379
for all of our students

00:23:39.379 --> 00:23:44.379
but we also have to resource
that issue, like we're real.

00:23:45.839 --> 00:23:48.039
And so we need to be looking at well,

00:23:48.039 --> 00:23:49.509
how do we deploy technology?

00:23:49.509 --> 00:23:50.779
How do we deploy teachers?

00:23:50.779 --> 00:23:53.549
I mean, our very best teachers working

00:23:53.549 --> 00:23:55.489
in our most challenging settings,

00:23:55.489 --> 00:23:58.349
do we have the right curriculum technology

00:23:58.349 --> 00:24:01.319
supports interventions to
close the achievement gap?

00:24:01.319 --> 00:24:05.169
So how do we think about
resources coupled with goals

00:24:05.169 --> 00:24:07.379
and objectives for students and families?

00:24:07.379 --> 00:24:11.709
And if you haven't, go find
out how your school is doing.

00:24:11.709 --> 00:24:14.229
Obviously, those will be old data points

00:24:14.229 --> 00:24:16.389
from last school year

00:24:16.389 --> 00:24:18.689
but, this school year we ought

00:24:18.689 --> 00:24:22.239
to be looking at, not
necessarily accountability based

00:24:22.239 --> 00:24:25.639
on a test, but where are the kids?

00:24:25.639 --> 00:24:29.169
How many minutes are they learning a day?

00:24:29.169 --> 00:24:30.059
What are the outcomes?

00:24:30.059 --> 00:24:32.469
We have to keep our eye on the ball

00:24:32.469 --> 00:24:35.809
and care enough to find
out, as I like to say,

00:24:35.809 --> 00:24:38.019
as opposed to kind of
this ostrich approach

00:24:38.019 --> 00:24:39.649
of burying our heads in the sand

00:24:39.649 --> 00:24:40.852
and hoping for the best.

00:24:41.989 --> 00:24:44.289
- Yeah, yeah, don't be
afraid of the information.

00:24:44.289 --> 00:24:46.429
Don't be afraid of the
message, so to speak.

00:24:46.429 --> 00:24:47.262
- Exactly.

00:24:49.479 --> 00:24:52.519
- Well, thank you so
much, Margaret, this was,

00:24:52.519 --> 00:24:53.659
I guess I really enjoyed this

00:24:53.659 --> 00:24:55.979
'cause a lot of what you said,

00:24:55.979 --> 00:24:59.769
resonated very strongly with me.

00:24:59.769 --> 00:25:01.929
But it's always great, especially

00:25:01.929 --> 00:25:03.229
in times of crisis like this

00:25:03.229 --> 00:25:06.669
to kind of, think about how
we could be doing things

00:25:06.669 --> 00:25:09.059
and how we can come
out of this in a better

00:25:09.059 --> 00:25:11.799
and better shape than we
maybe we even entered it.

00:25:11.799 --> 00:25:12.729
So thank you so much.

00:25:12.729 --> 00:25:14.959
- Don't waste the crisis, as they say.

00:25:14.959 --> 00:25:16.849
- Don't waste the crisis.

00:25:16.849 --> 00:25:18.769
Thank you, thank you for joining us.

00:25:18.769 --> 00:25:20.219
- Thanks Sal, appreciate you.

00:25:22.019 --> 00:25:23.249
- So thanks, everyone,

00:25:23.249 --> 00:25:25.889
for joining today, as
always, or not always,

00:25:25.889 --> 00:25:28.099
but especially today thank you.

00:25:28.099 --> 00:25:29.379
It was a great conversation

00:25:29.379 --> 00:25:30.939
with Secretary Spellings.

00:25:30.939 --> 00:25:33.619
It's always great to talk
to national leaders who,

00:25:33.619 --> 00:25:35.929
I think are really
thinking about the future

00:25:35.929 --> 00:25:38.629
in a way that's going
to best serve students

00:25:38.629 --> 00:25:41.239
and best serve teachers and parents

00:25:41.239 --> 00:25:45.809
and thanks for joining
and, we got to stay tuned.

00:25:45.809 --> 00:25:47.889
I'm also keeping track of
what's going on in the election.

00:25:47.889 --> 00:25:49.459
That's the other
interesting thing going on

00:25:49.459 --> 00:25:51.189
in everyone's lives right now.

00:25:51.189 --> 00:25:53.039
But we will see you next week.

00:25:53.039 --> 00:25:53.979
So please join us for

